WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.320 --> 00:00:03.960
- [Man Voiceover] We have
been called the Indians.

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:06.127
We are the Halluci Nation.

00:00:07.121 --> 00:00:10.621
(electronic tribal music)

00:00:12.719 --> 00:00:15.081
- [Narrator] Tribal music, club music

00:00:15.081 --> 00:00:18.081
mixed together and made by these guys.

00:00:18.081 --> 00:00:19.491
- [Man Voiceover] I'm DJ NDN.

00:00:19.491 --> 00:00:20.324
- [Man Voiceover] I'm 2oolman.

00:00:20.324 --> 00:00:21.382
- [Man Voiceover] I'm Bear Witness

00:00:21.382 --> 00:00:23.882
and we are A Tribe Called Red.

00:00:29.761 --> 00:00:32.030
- We remix traditional pow wow music

00:00:32.030 --> 00:00:34.447
with contemporary club music.

00:00:35.579 --> 00:00:36.978
- [Narrator] First nations pow wow music

00:00:36.978 --> 00:00:39.529
originated in the North American Plains.

00:00:39.529 --> 00:00:41.299
Traditionally it consists of a large drum

00:00:41.299 --> 00:00:43.649
with singers and dancers around it.

00:00:43.649 --> 00:00:46.419
- Essentially, pow wow music's
traditional dance music.

00:00:46.419 --> 00:00:48.507
That's why we ended up doing what we do.

00:00:48.507 --> 00:00:50.237
We're using our music now

00:00:50.237 --> 00:00:52.158
in much the same way
that pow wow was used,

00:00:52.158 --> 00:00:54.491
to create a gathering place.

00:00:55.749 --> 00:00:57.769
- [Narrator] And to A Tribe Called Red

00:00:57.769 --> 00:01:00.739
pow wow music isn't just an adopted style.

00:01:00.739 --> 00:01:03.249
It's a significant part of their heritage.

00:01:03.249 --> 00:01:06.110
- I'm Anishinaabe from
Nipissing First Nations.

00:01:06.110 --> 00:01:09.317
I grew up around pow wows
and going to pow wows.

00:01:09.317 --> 00:01:11.019
They're big cultural gatherings.

00:01:11.019 --> 00:01:13.878
- The significance of us
using pow wow in our music

00:01:13.878 --> 00:01:16.129
is just a way to create something

00:01:16.129 --> 00:01:17.678
that represents Indigenous people

00:01:17.678 --> 00:01:19.818
and that Indigenous people can relate to.

00:01:19.818 --> 00:01:21.198
(cheering)

00:01:21.198 --> 00:01:24.928
(electronic tribal music)

00:01:24.928 --> 00:01:26.990
- [Narrator] Their shows are
a cultural fusion experience

00:01:26.990 --> 00:01:28.389
combining more than just sound

00:01:28.389 --> 00:01:30.339
but visuals too.

00:01:30.339 --> 00:01:31.998
- We ended up hooking up with some people

00:01:31.998 --> 00:01:34.889
who were doing the same thing
we were doing with dance.

00:01:34.889 --> 00:01:36.110
They're a Native dance crew.

00:01:36.110 --> 00:01:37.198
They're breakers.

00:01:37.198 --> 00:01:38.539
But they mix it with pow wow.

00:01:38.539 --> 00:01:40.196
So it's that same kind of fusion.

00:01:40.196 --> 00:01:41.289
- [Narrator] And it's that combination

00:01:41.289 --> 00:01:43.038
of contemporary and tradition

00:01:43.038 --> 00:01:44.847
that's introducing Indigenous culture

00:01:44.847 --> 00:01:46.997
to a wider audience.

00:01:46.997 --> 00:01:48.358
- I think when we started

00:01:48.358 --> 00:01:50.310
if you asked your average
non-Indigenous person

00:01:50.310 --> 00:01:51.318
in Canada

00:01:51.318 --> 00:01:52.967
that if they had known
what pow wow music was

00:01:52.967 --> 00:01:55.638
they would shrug and say, "No".

00:01:55.638 --> 00:01:57.979
Now, I think that's begun to change.

00:01:57.979 --> 00:02:01.479
(electronic tribal music)

